  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1150 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nFor the third consecutive evening, the decaying remnants of a \nloosely organized convective cluster are spread out over portions of \nwest-central and southwest KS, with an outflow boundary racing \neastward. Showers and thunderstorms will continue to weaken over the \nnext few hours, and the rest of the overnight period will be quiet \nwith lows dropping into the low 60s northwest to the low 70s \nsoutheast. Daytime Sunday, short range ensembles show an upper level \nridge initially over the southeastern CONUS will retrograde to the \nsouthern plains. However, 500-mb heights will remain nearly\nstatic over southwest KS, and with multiple nights of significant\nprecipitation, afternoon temperatures will be below normal with\nhighs in the 80s for most locations outside of our southeast \nzones where low 90s will be achieved. Convective activity Sunday\nevening will be somewhat muted compared to the previous few \nnights, with all HREF members showing thunderstorms develop in \nsoutheast CO, but weaken as they approach the KS border. A rogue\nsevere wind gust cannot be ruled out if thunderstorms do \nsurvive long enough, but chances are low (<20%).\n\nDaytime Monday, ensembles agree the upper level ridge will flatten \nout and encompass the entire southern CONUS by 06Z Tuesday. This \nprocess will have little noteworthy impact on southwest KS, as only \nstrong solar insolation will drive a minor uptick in temperatures \nwith highs in the mid-80s to low 90s. Thunderstorm activity also \nappears unlikely as the majority of the HREF members keep our area \ndry, but a stray thunderstorm or two cannot be ruled out favoring \nour western zones.\n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest the upper level ridge over the southern CONUS will gradually \nbuild northward over the western CONUS. This synoptic pattern \nevolution will engender a slow warming trend through the end of the \nweek, coinciding with decreasing precipitation chances. At the \nmoment, Wednesday evening carries the highest probability of \nrainfall as an upper level shortwave trough and attendant cold front \npass through the central plains. NBM paints pops in the chance \ncategory (25-54%) throughout much of southwest KS, and these will \nlikely increase as ensemble members converge on a solution.",
